## Prior art, which this does not restate

Three layers already cover most of this. Where one applies, it is the authority.

**[PEP 257](https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/) — docstring form.** The summary
is a phrase ending in a period, in the imperative: *"It prescribes the function
or method's effect as a command ('Do this', 'Return that'), not as a
description."* It must not restate the signature. ruff enforces most of this as
`D401`, `D402`, `D404`, `D205` and `D400`, so never flag it by hand.

**[ASD-STE100](https://www.asd-ste100.org/STE_faq.html) — Simplified Technical
English.** A controlled language from aerospace maintenance documentation: 53
writing rules and a dictionary of about 900 approved words, current edition
January 2025. Rules 1 and 2 below are STE's, not this document's — one topic per
sentence, and vertical lists for complex text. STE also caps sentences at
roughly 20 words for procedures and 25 for description, and caps paragraphs at
six sentences. Take those as guidance rather than as a gate; STE itself warns
that dropping words to hit a limit creates ambiguity instead of clarity.

**Google or NumPy sections** for arguments, returns and raises, whichever the
project configures.

## What is left over

Rules 4, 5 and 6 are the ones no existing standard names. PEP 257 describes how
people got docstrings wrong in 2001. STE was written for human technical authors
describing physical procedures. Neither anticipated prose that quotes another
docstring back at the reader, narrates what the code used to do, builds
rhetorical scaffolding that sounds like insight, or writes a sentence about what
the section is doing.

Those are common in machine-written prose, and they are the reason this file
exists. Rules 1 to 3 are restated here only so the whole set reads in one place.

The test: read it aloud. If you run out of breath, or lose track of what the
subject was, rewrite it.

### 1. One idea per sentence

A sentence with two subordinate clauses is two sentences. A semicolon joining
two independent clauses is usually two sentences as well.

> **No:** "It is the entry point to the two stages below it -- neither of those
> is a thing a caller invokes, and until this landed nothing joined up what they
> returned."
>
> **Yes:** "Neither stage below is called directly. This is."

### 2. Cases in a list

Where there are several cases, list them. Prose that rules each one out in turn
forces the reader to hold the eliminated ones in their head.

> **No:** "An empty value cannot occur here, because the caller already rejected
> it; what is left is either a value the store has not loaded yet, or one a
> later pass will compute."
>
> **Yes:** "There are two cases: the store has not loaded the value yet, or a
> later pass computes it."

### 3. Reasons only where needed

Give a reason where it is not obvious, in its own short paragraph. A reason
attached to every statement buries the one that mattered.

> **No:** "Sorted after filtering, not before: two entries that differ only in
> case are one entry, and only the folded key can say that they are."
>
> **Yes:** "Entries are sorted after filtering. Two that differ only in case are
> one entry, and only the folded key shows that."

### 4. No narration, quotation or history

Do not restate the code underneath. Do not quote another docstring. Do not
record what the code used to do — that is the commit message's job, and the
commit message is where someone will look for it.

> **No:** "Then the case the module docstring promises and this used to get
> wrong: an empty result means the answer is unknown, never that it is zero."
>
> **Yes:** "An empty result means the answer is unknown, not that it is zero."

### 5. No rhetorical filler

Cut the shapes that sound like insight and carry none: "which is the point",
"not X but Y", "and that is exactly", "it is worth noting", "worth keeping
sharp", a second em-dash aside hung off the first.

> **No:** "They are different altitudes, not competing names for one thing."
>
> **Yes:** "One is a stage, the other is what calls it."

### 6. No talking about the document

Do not describe what a section is doing, why it exists, or what it fixes. The
reader wants the content, not an account of the content.

> **No:** "Terms this document assumes. Defined here because they were used
> before being defined, which is the gap this section closes."
>
> **Yes:** *(nothing — delete the sentence and start the glossary)*

## What this is not

Not a length limit. A long docstring a reader can follow is fine; a short one
built from three nested clauses is not.

Not a ban on explaining. Rule 3 asks for reasons where they are needed. A
decision that took a day to reach, or an assumption nothing checks, is worth a
paragraph. Restating the line below it is not.
